Restore the seat keyboard when a keyboard group is destroyed.

dwl gives each virtual keyboard its own KeyboardGroup. createkeyboardgroup()
points the seat keyboard at the new group. destroykeyboardgroup() destroys that
group but never points the seat back at the physical keyboard group, so the seat
keeps a dead keyboard pointer.

A Wayland client that connects in that state receives no keymap. wezterm then
aborts with "no keymap" at window/src/os/wayland/keyboard.rs:113. A terminal
keybinding therefore opens no window at all.

keypress() repairs the seat, but only for keys that it does not handle. A
keybinding returns before that line, so a key that spawns a client never
repairs the seat. A key that fuzzel or a shell consumes does repair it. That is
why a terminal starts from fuzzel but not from Mod+Shift+Return.

--- a/dwl.c
+++ b/dwl.c
@@ -1676,11 +1676,24 @@
 destroykeyboardgroup(struct wl_listener *listener, void *data)
 {
 	KeyboardGroup *group = wl_container_of(listener, group, destroy);
+	/* Read the seat keyboard before the group goes away. */
+	int held_seat = wlr_seat_get_keyboard(seat) == &group->wlr_group->keyboard;
+	int is_main = group == kb_group;
+
 	wl_event_source_remove(group->key_repeat_source);
 	UNLISTEN(&group->key);
 	UNLISTEN(&group->modifiers);
 	UNLISTEN(&group->destroy);
 	wlr_keyboard_group_destroy(group->wlr_group);
+
+	/* Give the seat a keyboard again. A virtual keyboard makes its own
+	 * group, and createkeyboardgroup() points the seat at that group. The
+	 * seat keeps a dead pointer when the group goes away. A client that
+	 * connects then gets no keymap. Skip this during cleanup(), because
+	 * there kb_group is the group that we destroy. */
+	if (held_seat && !is_main)
+		wlr_seat_set_keyboard(seat, &kb_group->wlr_group->keyboard);
+
 	free(group);
 }
 
